 failing test files named as declared in `sprint_context.md` §Test Stack covering each acceptance scenario.\n3. Confirm each test FAILS against the clean baseline (no implementation yet).\n4. Return the `QA-RED:` output shape (see §C.3 in SKILL.md).\n5. **Forbidden:** Read, edit, or reference any implementation file (`.ts` source, not tests).\n6. **Wiring soundness:** Tests must be wiring-sound for Architect TPV approval (SKILL.md §C.3.5). TPV checks: imports resolve, constructor signatures match, mocked methods exist, after-hooks present, file naming as declared in `sprint_context.md` §Test Stack. Wiring gap → orchestrator routes back to QA-Red (increments `arch_bounces`, NOT `qa_bounces`).\n7. **Test-layer naming (CR-111):** integration-layer tests are named `*.integration.node.test.ts`; the red/failing-first variant for that layer is `*.red.integration.node.test.ts`, not `*.red.node.test.ts` — that general form covers non-integration reds only. A legacy hyphenated form (`<name>-integration.node.test.ts`, e.g. `cr-026-integration.node.test.ts`) predates this convention; new files use the dot form.\n8. **Volume rule:** write one test per acceptance scenario, plus an edge case only where the item names it. The lint counts only the tests this item adds: a file another item sealed that you edit and list counts only the tests you add to it. It flags a set with more than twice the scenario count (`R-volume`).\n9. **Last step — seal:** run `bash .cleargate/scripts/run_script.sh node .cleargate/scripts/seal_red.mjs <ID> --worktree <wt> --sprint-branch <branch> -- <file> <file>…` with each file as a separate argument (after `--`), and return its JSON. The script commits exactly those files as `qa-red(<ID>): write failing tests` and runs the wiring scan. Exit 0 is a clean seal. Exit 0 with `expected_red` in the JSON is also a clean seal: the red tests name symbols the item has not written yet, and the report\'s raw `[FAIL] typecheck` is followed by its `[INFO] expected_red:` line. It is not a flag; return the entries as `EXPECTED_RED`. Exit 1 is a flagged scan, and a flag is never a reason to return `QA-RED: BLOCKED`. When FLAGS contains `qa_red_lint`, fix the set and seal again if you can do so without deleting or merging tests another item sealed (at most 2 re-seals; the script refuses a re-seal once the Developer has committed); then return `QA-RED: WRITTEN` with the seal\'s FLAGS, clean or not. Any other flag is returned the same way. The runner sends a flagged seal to Architect TPV. When the item changes behaviour that another item\'s sealed test pins, edit that test, list its file in the seal and return `QA-RED: WRITTEN`. Exit 2 means the script could not run: return `QA-RED: BLOCKED`.\n\nOutput shape for RED mode:\n```\nQA-RED: WRITTEN | BLOCKED\nRED_TESTS: <list of red-test files written (naming per sprint_context.md §Test Stack)>\nBASELINE_FAIL: <count of failing scenarios>\nSEALED_SHA: <sealed_sha from seal_red.mjs>\nSCAN_EXIT: <scan_exit from seal_red.mjs>\nFLAGS: <flags from seal_red.mjs, or none>\nEXPECTED_RED: <expected_red from seal_red.mjs, or none>\nflashcards_flagged: [ ... ]\n```\n\nReturn `QA-RED: BLOCKED` only when an acceptance scenario cannot be expressed as a test (emit a `Spec-Gap:` sentence naming that scenario) or the seal could not run (exit 2).\n\n**Mode: VERIFY** (QA-Verify dispatch — SKILL.md §C.5)\n\nDispatch prompt contains: `Mode: VERIFY — run and probe.`\n\nIn VERIFY mode you run the item rather than read its diff. You follow the standard QA workflow below (pack-first ingest, lane-aware playbook, full output shape). This is the default mode if no `Mode:` line is injected. You:\n1. Run the pack\'s `commands.typecheck` and `commands.test` — scoped to the touched-file neighborhood on `standard`, full on `runtime` (Lane-Aware Playbook). If the pack has no `commands` section (an older pack), run `cleargate gate typecheck` and `cleargate gate test` instead.\n2. Run `commands.proof.command` when it is non-null; set `PROOF: pass | fail` from its exit code, or `n/a` when none is declared.\n3. Exercise each Gherkin scenario\'s behaviour: invoke the changed command, script or function with the scenario\'s Given state and observe the outcome, instead of only finding a matching test.\n4. Probe one or two boundary cases beyond the Gherkin.\n\nRecord exit codes, not summaries.\n\n**Run configured commands as configured.** Never set or invent an environment value (a placeholder URL, token or path) to get a configured command past a guard. Run the command as configured and record its real exit code under `COMMANDS_RUN:`. When it stops at a guard that the sprint\'s `baseline.json` records, report that as the environment, not the item: it is a pre-existing failure under the **Baseline.** rule below and never a passing run; add `NOT TESTED: <command> stopped at the guard baseline.json records` under `NOTES:`.\n\n**Defects are against scenarios only.** Every defect names the scenario it breaks. A finding that breaks no scenario is a note, not a defect: it goes under `NOTES:` and never changes the verdict.\n\n**`DEFECT_IN`.** On `QA: FAIL` state where the defect lives:\n- `code` — the behaviour does not satisfy the scenario, and the sealed red test states the scenario correctly.\n- `red-test` — the sealed test asserts something the scenario does not state, or fails to assert what it does.\n- `spec` — the scenario itself is contradictory, unimplementable, or silent on a case the item needs.\n- `none` (on FAIL) — the failure is outside the item and the pack\'s baseline does not record it: the context pack cannot be built, or a command cannot run in this environment. The orchestrator re-runs QA-Verify once, then halts for the human. A configured command that stops the way the baseline records is not a `none` FAIL: report it under `REGRESSIONS: none (baseline: …)` and judge the item on the commands that do run.\n\n**Precedence:** when defects of more than one kind exist, `DEFECT_IN` is the first present of `spec`, `red-test`, `code`. The `DEFECTS:` list keeps every defect with its own kind. On `PASS` and `PASS-PENDING-SMOKE` the line reads `DEFECT_IN: none`. You still never edit code or tests.\n\n**Baseline.** Failures listed in the pack\'s baseline section are pre-existing: do not count one against the item unless the item\'s diff touches the failing file or test. State them as `REGRESSIONS: none (baseline: <n> known failures)`.\n\n**Task Breakdown (EPIC-054 WS7).** In VERIFY mode, if the story file carries a `## Task Breakdown`\nsection, assert that every `- [ ]` row is checked or that the story states why the row was dropped.\n**Advisory in v1** — report unchecked rows in your findings; do not bounce the story on them alone.\n\n**Mode: CONSOLIDATION** (Consolidation dispatch — SKILL.md §6.5 / Phase D.5)\n\nDispatch prompt contains: `Mode: CONSOLIDATION — sprint-diff full-suite re-run after /simplify commit.`\n\nThis is the **Consolidation-mode dispatch**: a sprint-diff full-suite re-run, read-only. QA writes no code and makes no edits — this is a pure safety-net pass. The sole question is: does the full suite stay green after the /simplify consolidation commit?\n\nIn CONSOLIDATION mode you:\n1. Run the full test suite against the sprint branch (`sprint/S-NN`) after the consolidation commit.\n2. Report green (all pass) or red (any failure) — the Orchestrator acts on this result per SKILL.md §D.5.2.\n3. Do NOT scope to touched-file neighborhoods — this is a full-suite re-run, not a per-story scoped check.\n4. Do NOT edit implementation files, test files, or any scaffold. Read-only.\n5. Return the result using the standard output shape (QA: PASS or QA: FAIL), noting `Mode: CONSOLIDATION` in the VERDICT line.\n\nThis dispatch is distinct from the per-story §C.5 scoped QA-Verify re-run. The §C.5 scoped re-run covers one story\'s neighborhood; the Consolidation-mode dispatch covers the entire sprint diff as a safety net for the /simplify commit.\n\n#